  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
    "lesson_core_move": "Derive the RLC circuit transfer function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s) by Laplace-transforming its differential equation and expressing the result as a rational s-domain input-output ratio for pole and stability analysis$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target object in this section is one transfer function ratio, not a long derivation. Every later step in this topic should reuse the same goal: from a differential equation, produce $H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}$ in one algebraic pass so stability analysis can be done directly from the denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the canonical base model from this topic and hold it fixed while students learn the method: $L C\\,\\frac{d^2 y(t)}{dt^2}+R C\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+y(t)=x(t)$. The only required action is to Laplace-transform each term and then collect powers of $Y(s)$ and $X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Under the zero-initial-condition convention used in this module, use these substitutions: $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'(t)\\}=sY(s)$, $\\mathcal{L}\\{y''(t)\\}=s^2Y(s)$, $\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}=Y(s)$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$. No extra initial-condition terms are carried in this setup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L C\\,s^2Y(s)+R C\\,sY(s)+Y(s)=X(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now isolate the ratio by dividing by the input-side factor: $H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}$. This is the one move students need to memorize before moving on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}=\\frac{1/(L C)}{s^2+(R/L)s+1/(L C)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The skill you should carry forward from this section is the denominator-first check: rewrite $H(s)$ in rational form, read the denominator $D(s)$, and use it for pole location work in the next steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Laplace-Transforming an RLC Differential Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Apply the Laplace transform to $L C\\,\\frac{d^2 y(t)}{dt^2}+R C\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+y(t)=x(t)$ under zero initial conditions and express the equation in terms of $Y(s)$ and $X(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue for this example is the differential equation itself. The action is the same conversion pattern as the previous step: map each time-domain term to its Laplace-domain form, set initial conditions to zero, and collect a single algebraic relation in $Y(s)$ and $X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\frac{d^2 y}{dt^2}\\}=s^2Y(s)-s y(0^-)-\\dot y(0^-)$ for the second-derivative term and $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\frac{dy}{dt}\\}=sY(s)-y(0^-)$ for the first-derivative term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the zero-initial-condition rule from this module: set $y(0^-)=0$ and $\\dot y(0^-)=0$, so the derivative transforms reduce to multiplication by $s$. Also use $\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}=Y(s)$ and $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L C\\left(s^2Y(s)-s y(0^-)-\\dot y(0^- )\\right)+R C\\left(sY(s)-y(0^-)\\right)+Y(s)=X(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L C\\,s^2Y(s)+R C\\,sY(s)+Y(s)=X(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Collect the transformed terms on one line and verify the result is purely algebraic: a single relation between $Y(s)$ and $X(s)$ with no lingering time-domain terms. The final transformed equation is the completed target for this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L C\\,s^2Y(s)+R C\\,sY(s)+Y(s)=X(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Isolating the Output-to-Input Ratio",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From the transformed relation $L C\\,s^2Y(s)+R C\\,sY(s)+Y(s)=X(s)$, isolate $\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}$ in one fraction.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue for this example is the transformed differential equation itself. The action is to isolate the system ratio form by turning one transformed equation into the explicit transfer expression $H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First, factor all left-side $Y(s)$ terms into one polynomial coefficient times $Y(s)$. This keeps the final ratio step purely mechanical.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(L C\\,s^2 + R C\\,s + 1)Y(s)=X(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, divide both sides by $X(s)$ to place $Y(s)$ over $X(s)$ and keep all input dependence in the denominator factor only.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That gives the core transfer ratio. Optionally normalize by dividing numerator and denominator by $L C$ to expose the standard second-order template when desired:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{1}{L C\\left(s^2+\\frac{R}{L}s+\\frac{1}{L C}\\right)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing an RLC Transfer Function as a Rational Function of s",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $H(s)=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}$ as $\\frac{K}{s^2+(R/L)s+1/(L C)}$ for $L=1$, $R=4$, $C=\\frac12$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the raw transfer form $H(s)=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}$. The rule is to divide through by the leading $s^2$ coefficient so the denominator is in standard second-order form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by dividing numerator and denominator by $L C$. This is legal because $L C\\neq 0$ for passive components, and it exposes the normalized template explicitly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{1}{L C\\,s^2+R C\\,s+1}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{L C}}{s^2+\\frac{R}{L}s+\\frac{1}{L C}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $L=1$, $R=4$, $C=\\frac12$. Then $L C=\\frac12$, so the normalized gain becomes $\\frac{1}{L C}=2$ and the denominator coefficients become $\\frac{R}{L}=4$ and $\\frac{1}{L C}=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{2}{s^2+4s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep both checkpoints. The unnormalized denominator is $\\frac12 s^2+2s+1$, and the standardized denominator is $s^2+4s+2$. Both are equivalent, but the latter is the form you reuse for pole and stability work.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "RLC Circuit Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Denominator Polynomial for Pole Analysis",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s^2+3s+2}$, state the characteristic polynomial and list the two poles.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the denominator in $H(s)$, since poles are defined by the values of $s$ where the transfer function denominator is zero. So we read $H(s)$ in denominator-first form and identify",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=s^2+3s+2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, factor this quadratic to expose poles directly as roots of $D(s)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2+3s+2=(s+1)(s+2),\\quad D(s)=0\\iff s=-1\\text{ or }s=-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both roots are negative real parts, so this pole candidate set signals a stable pattern in later LTI stability checkpoints.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Checkpoint statement to reuse: characteristic polynomial $D(s)=s^2+3s+2$, poles $\\{-1,-2\\}$, stable-sign pattern $(\\Re\\{s\\}<0)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
